            text: "At sixty-two, a retired engineer enrolled in her first ever art class, admitting to the rest of the beginners, most of them decades younger, that she had always been prone to assuming it was too late to start something new. Six months later she was making a point of attending twice a week, insisting it was the most demanding thing she had done in years, harder in some ways than the engineering degree she had completed four decades earlier.\n\nHer story captures something that educators increasingly emphasise: that learning is not a stage of life to be completed and then left behind, but a habit that either continues or quietly stalls into stagnation. An autodidact long before online courses existed, she had taught herself to code in her forties purely out of curiosity, an insatiable one, by her own description, that made her uncomfortable with the idea of ever fully stopping.\n\nNot everyone finds this comfortable advice. It is reasonable to ask whether lifelong learning, as it is often marketed, places an unfair burden on people already exhausted by work, family and the ordinary demands of simply getting through a week, implying that anyone who is not proactively retraining in their spare time is somehow falling behind. There is a real difference between genuine curiosity and a kind of anxious, compulsory self-improvement that treats rest as a failure to optimise oneself, and conflating the two does lifelong learning no favours.\n\nAdaptability, however, does appear to matter more than it once did, given how quickly entire industries can be reshaped by new technology. Workers who are prone to treating their current skills as permanent risk discovering, sometimes abruptly, that those skills have quietly become less relevant, while those who make a point of recalibrating their knowledge every few years tend to adjust with considerably less disruption. This is not the same as suggesting people should be perpetually dissatisfied with where they are; it is closer to suggesting that curiosity, kept gently alive, tends to make unavoidable change far less frightening when it eventually arrives.\n\nWhat made the retired engineer's art class meaningful was not, in the end, the specific skill of painting, which she readily admits she is still fairly mediocre at. It was coming to terms with being a beginner again, something adults are rarely asked to practise once their careers settle into a comfortable rhythm, and something teenagers, paradoxically, are asked to do constantly and rarely thanked for.\n\nWhat this suggests for someone finishing years of structured study is not that the learning stops here, nor that it must continue at the same relentless pace indefinitely. It is, more modestly, that staying curious, proactive about new skills and willing to recalibrate when something stops working, is less a burden to be endured than a form of insurance against the quiet stagnation that eventually catches up with anyone who assumes they have already learned enough.",
